Content:
  1  ---
  2  name: figure-reproduce
  3  description: Reproduce a target figure from a dataset — regenerate a published/reference plot (e.g. a scatter, line/bar chart, heatmap, UMAP, dotplot, violin, or spatial map from a paper) using the coding agent and matching its panels, layout, and color encoding. Use when the user asks to "reproduce", "recreate", "replicate", or "remake" a figure, or to match a figure from a paper/notebook. Covers self-evaluation of the result and a bounded reflect-and-retry repair loop when the first attempt is off.
  4  applies_to: [coding_agent]
  5  status: enable
  6  strict: true
  7  ---
  8  
  9  # Figure Reproduction
 10  Reproduce a reference figure from supplied data, match its scientific
 11  content and visual encoding, verify the result against the target, and
 12  repair meaningful mismatches.
 13  
 14  ## When to use
 15  
 16  - The user says "reproduce / recreate / replicate / remake figure X", "match this
 17    plot", or "make the same figure as the paper".
 18  - The target may be a **paper figure** (recruit the `pdf_reader` agent, or use
 19    `scripts/extract_pdf_figure.py` to pull the figure image + caption), a notebook
 20    plot, an image/PDF path, a description, or a prior output figure.
 21  - **Not** for designing a brand-new figure with no reference (that's ordinary
 22    plotting), and never to **fabricate** data to match a figure — reproduce only
 23    what the data legitimately supports.
 24  
 25  ## Inputs
 26  
 27  - Target figure (required) — the figure or panel to reproduce.
 28  - Dataset (required) — the data from which the figure should be regenerated.
 29  - Method description (optional) — text describing how the target figure was generated, such as a paper Methods section, figure caption, analysis description, or user-provided instructions. Use it to recover preprocessing, algorithms, parameters, data subsets, and plotting choices.
 30  - Method plot / workflow figure (optional) — a schematic, flowchart, or other figure describing the analysis procedure that produced the target. Interpret it together with the method description to reconstruct the intended workflow.
 31  
 32  If either target figure or dataset is unavailable, report the missing requirement rather than fabricating it.
 33  
 34  ## Required outputs
 35  
 36  - `project/outputs/figures/<name>.png`
 37  - vector figure when appropriate
 38  - `project/outputs/tables/plotted_data.csv`
 39  - `project/outputs/tables/colormap.yaml`
 40  - `project/outputs/tables/compare_metrics.json`
 41  - `project/outputs/reports/<name>_repro_note.md`
 42  
 43  Every render and every comparison is saved under an attempt suffix
 44  (`<name>_attempt1.png`, `compare_figures_attempt1.png`,
 45  `compare_figures_geometry_attempt1.png`, `compare_metrics_attempt1.json`, …) so
 46  the repair trajectory is auditable. Copy the accepted attempt to the plain
 47  `<name>` above; do not overwrite an earlier attempt.
 48  
 49  ## Invariants
 50  
 51  1. Generate the figure by running analysis/plotting code on the data.
 52     Never copy, decode, download, or synthesize the target image.
 53  2. Do not guess palette, category order, labels, orientation, or
 54     colormap direction when they can be measured.
 55  3. Never silently substitute a default palette.
 56  4. Every reproduction must be compared against the target.
 57  5. Structural mismatches must be fixed before completion.
 58  6. Maximum 5 render attempts and 1 data/environment re-preparation.
 59  7. Any unresolved mismatch or unsupported feature must be recorded in
 60     the repro note.
 61  
 62  ## Completion Gate
 63  
 64  Do not report success unless all are true:
 65  
 66  - [ ] figure rendered from supplied data
 67  - [ ] plotted-data CSV written
 68  - [ ] palette provenance tier verified and reconciled with plotted categories
 69  - [ ] automated comparison executed after every render, each saved under its
 70        own attempt suffix
 71  - [ ] no unresolved structural defects
 72  - [ ] ≤5 render attempts
 73  - [ ] residual differences documented
 74  - [ ] repro note written
 75  
 76  
 77  ## Workflow
 78  
 79  The bundled scripts live under `project/skills/figure-reproduce/scripts/`. Run
 80  them by full path, e.g.:
 81  
 82  ```
 83  python3 project/skills/figure-reproduce/scripts/build_colormap.py --help
 84  ```
 85  
 86  A bare filename will not resolve. Run each script rather than reimplementing it.
 87  
 88  ### 1. Acquire and inspect the target
 89  
 90  Obtain the target image/panel and caption.
 91  
 92  Record:
 93  - plot primitive and panel count
 94  - orientation/aspect
 95  - background/underlay
 96  - categorical vs continuous encoding
 97  - legend/colorbar, or whether category color lives in the marks
 98  - visible text
 99  - marker style
100  - unusual annotations
101  
102  Confirm that the target crop contains only the panel being reproduced. Re-crop a
103  paper-page image that includes neighboring panels, captions, or orientation aids;
104  otherwise record the extra content and discount it during comparison.
105  
106  ### 2. Inspect the dataset
107  
108  Identify the exact data subset and fields required for the figure:
109  coordinates/embedding, category/value field, genes/features, and
110  relevant metadata.
111  
112  Record categorical values and their plotting order for palette reconciliation.
113  
114  If required information is absent, stop and report the blocker.
115  
116  ### 3. Measure the reference
117  
118  Run `scripts/extract_reference_spec.py`. For long legends, use
119  `--max-legend-entries`; `--max-colors` controls only the dominant-color summary.
120  
121  Resolve:
122  - palette / colormap
123  - category order
124  - visible labels
125  - orientation and panel geometry
126  
127  Treat measured values as the plotting specification.
128  
129  ### 4. Resolve palette
130  
131  Run `scripts/build_colormap.py`.
132  
133  Allowed sources, highest priority first:
134  1. supplied palette
135  2. dataset metadata
136  3. reference legend
137  4. reliable reference pixels
138  
139  An explicit supplied palette remains authoritative. When a named reference
140  legend materially disagrees, the script retains the supplied colors and records
141  the conflict for the repro note.
142  
143  If no trustworthy palette can be resolved, report a constraint
144  violation instead of silently using defaults.
145  
146  The script measures colours; you own the pairing. Legends and datasets often
147  disagree — different spellings, a class the figure shows but the data lacks, a
148  scale bar counted as a swatch. When the script warns that counts differ or that
149  categories went unmatched, resolve it yourself by name and record the decision:
150  assign a neutral grey to categories the figure does not distinguish, and say in
151  the note which are measured and which are not.
152  
153  Every category must end up with a colour, and no two categories may share one
154  unless you state that it is deliberate.
155  
156  Confirm the written file with `scripts/build_colormap.py --verify`.
157  
158  ### 5. Render attempt 1
159  
160  Generate the figure using the measured specification.
161  
162  Match:
163  - data subset
164  - primitive
165  - panel layout
166  - palette/order
167  - labels
168  - orientation/aspect
169  - background/underlay
170  - legend styling (marker shape, size, ordering, placement)
171  
172  Before drawing, assert that the resolved palette covers the post-filter table
173  actually passed to the plotting call:
174  
175  ```python
176  missing = sorted(set(plotted[category_col]) - set(palette))
177  assert not missing, f"no resolved colour for: {missing}"
178  ```
179  
180  Save as `<name>_attempt1.png`, plus the plotting code and plotted-data table.
181  
182  ### 6. Compare attempt 1
183  
184  Run `scripts/compare_figures.py` on `<name>_attempt1.png` with `--attempt 1` and
185  save its JSON as `compare_metrics_attempt1.json`. The script writes distinct
186  `compare_figures_attempt1.png` and `compare_figures_geometry_attempt1.png` files
187  beside the reproduction by default. The first is a three-panel comparison:
188  target | reproduction | 50% overlay. The second is letterboxed and preserves
189  each image's shape.
190  
191  ```bash
192  python3 project/skills/figure-reproduce/scripts/compare_figures.py \
193    <target.png> project/outputs/figures/<name>_attempt1.png \
194    --attempt 1 --json > project/outputs/tables/compare_metrics_attempt1.json
195  ```
196  
197  Check both:
198  - content fidelity — the similarity metrics
199  - structural fidelity — `pass2_geometry.findings`; proceed only on `clean: true`
200  
201  If the geometry comparison remains poor, first check whether the target crop
202  contains content the reproduction intentionally omits before changing plotting code.
203  
204  Then inspect both comparison images, using the overlay panel to check alignment,
205  and explicitly list the differences you
206  can see, at minimum:
207  - plot content
208  - panel count
209  - orientation
210  - axes
211  - category set/order
212  - palette / colormap
213  - labels
214  - legend (presence, entries, order, marker shape)
215  - background
216  - aspect ratio
217  - marker size/density
218  - layout
219  
220  ### 7. Diagnose and propose changes
221  
222  Before modifying the code, produce a short repair plan:
223  
224  - observed difference
225  - likely cause
226  - proposed change
227  
228  Prioritize structural differences before aesthetic differences.
229  
230  Example:
231  
232  | Difference | Likely cause | Change |
233  |---|---|---|
234  | vertically flipped | image coordinates use opposite y direction | invert y-axis |
235  | category colors mismatched | palette/order mapping incorrect | rebuild category-color mapping |
236  | target is portrait | incorrect figsize | match target aspect ratio |
237  
238  ### 8. Repair and render attempt 2
239  
240  Apply the proposed changes in one batched repair pass.
241  
242  Re-run the plotting code and save the new figure as `<name>_attempt2.png`.
243  
244  Do not modify the target or fabricate data to improve similarity.
245  
246  ### 9. Compare attempt 2
247  
248  Run `scripts/compare_figures.py` on `<name>_attempt2.png` with `--attempt 2`,
249  saving its JSON as `compare_metrics_attempt2.json`; keep the default distinct
250  `compare_figures_attempt2.png` and `compare_figures_geometry_attempt2.png` names.
251  
252  Inspect the new metrics and diff images.
253  
254  Explicitly check:
255  - which previously identified differences were fixed
256  - which differences remain
257  - whether any new defects were introduced
258  
259  The repaired figure is not considered verified until this second
260  comparison has been completed.
261  
262  ### 10. Optional final repair
263  
264  If meaningful differences remain and the cause is clear:
265  
266  1. propose the remaining change,
267  2. repair,
268  3. render attempt 3 (`<name>_attempt3.png`),
269  4. run `scripts/compare_figures.py` on it with `--attempt 3`, saving all outputs
270     with the matching suffix,
271  5. inspect the result.
272  
273  Repeat the loop.
274  
275  Maximum: 5 total renders.
276  
277  Do not perform more renders without subsequently running
278  `scripts/compare_figures.py`.
279  
280  Stop when the remaining mismatch is caused by unavailable or different data and
281  no longer responds to plotting changes. Record the limitation instead of spending
282  the remaining attempts on it.
283  
284  
285  ### 11. Finalize
286  
287  Copy the accepted attempt byte-for-byte to the plain `<name>` filenames; never
288  re-render during finalization. Then verify all required artifacts exist. Keep
289  every attempt on disk.
290  
291  Write the repro note with:
292  - data fields/subset
293  - important parameters
294  - palette source tier, reconciliation result, and any unrecovered color
295  - verification result
296  - number of attempts, and the metric trajectory across them
297  - category vocabulary differences (naming, extra/missing classes)
298  - target-crop content not reproduced
299  - deviations or blockers
300  
301  Do not report completion unless all structural defects are resolved or
302  explicitly identified as unsupported by the available data.
303  
304  ## References
305  
306  Read only when needed:
307  
308  - `references/panel-geometry.md`
309  - `references/fidelity-check.md`
310  - `references/reflect-and-retry.md`
311  - `references/debugging-playbook.md`
312  - `references/domain-recipes.md`

